WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.540
Speaker 1: Today we're going to be speaking
with Alana Newhouse, who's the founder and

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:06.400
editor in chief of Tablet magazine.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:12.019
We're going to be talking about a term broken
ism, which I think is super interesting.

00:00:12.019 --> 00:00:15.940
And it sort of puts a different perspective
on what, you know, maybe Alana in our program

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:18.810
we often see is like a left right political
divide only.

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:20.140
And that's the analysis we make.

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:22.080
And we sort of don't go any further.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:26.619
You talk about broken ism and status quo ism.

00:00:26.619 --> 00:00:31.099
Talk a little bit about how you first thought
of this framework and what you mean by it.

00:00:31.099 --> 00:00:32.820
Speaker 2: Sure.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:38.280
So two years ago, I wrote a piece called Everything
Is Broken.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:43.719
In it, I tried to articulate what felt to
me like an experience that not only I was

00:00:43.719 --> 00:00:45.371
having, but a lot of my readers were having.

00:00:45.371 --> 00:00:50.870
A lot of people who I knew were having, which
is the sense that a lot of the institutions

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:54.940
in American life and society that we have
to interact with, whether that's our kids

00:00:54.940 --> 00:01:03.530
schools or our health insurance companies
or the IRS, felt like they were not functioning

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:04.530
properly.

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:08.030
They felt broken in the wake of that piece.

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:10.221
I got a tremendous response to it.

00:01:10.221 --> 00:01:12.060
It was really incredibly gratifying.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:16.820
And one of the greatest parts of it was that
I ended up getting emails from a bunch of

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:21.079
people who became regular correspondence with
me.

00:01:21.079 --> 00:01:23.530
And one of them became a friend.

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:24.700
And his name is Ryan.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:29.280
And I talk about this in the beginning of
the follow up essay on Broken ISM, where at

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.260
one moment Ryan looked at me and said, I don't
even know what I am.

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:38.470
I don't know if I'm a Republican or a Democrat
or if I'm a liberal or conservative.

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:43.870
All I know is that I believe in your piece,
in the basic premise of your piece, which

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:46.310
is that things feel broken.

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:49.440
And so I guess I believe in broken ism.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:57.200
And that kind of articulated an idea for me
that I thought was very clarifying because

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.729
it felt to me like that actually was the thing
that people believed from all over the spectrum.

00:02:02.729 --> 00:02:08.849
And so I tried to write a second piece that
articulated what I thought a brokenness perspective

00:02:08.849 --> 00:02:10.069
on the world entailed.

00:02:10.069 --> 00:02:15.130
Speaker 1: You know, sometimes when I'll have
a more detailed conversation with folks who

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:19.080
have this general idea that there's a lot
of things that aren't working, but they might

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:23.209
identify as being on the political left or
on the political right.

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:27.890
One of the things I've experienced is that
when you really get details from them about

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:31.580
what are the ways in which some of these systems
aren't working?

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:34.269
And what's your instinct about the solution?

00:02:34.269 --> 00:02:39.989
It seems to align much more closely with what
would be considered a left wing view.

00:02:39.989 --> 00:02:45.700
Now, that may be my bias and who I'm talking
to, but very often it will be the the revelation

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:51.250
I have that there's a lot of discontent with
many systems, but a lot of people don't seem

00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:57.110
to realize that the solutions they are gravitating
towards are actually more aligned with one

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:59.050
side of political spectrum than the other.

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:02.730
And that's sort of like a concern to me that
people aren't acknowledging that.

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:06.120
Do do you experience the same thing when you
talk to some of these folks, or is my view

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:08.850
maybe partial or biased?

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:14.080
Speaker 2: What I think I probably experience
something a little different from you, but

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.640
what I guess I would say is, is that one of
the things that people talk to me about is

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:24.220
not only the solutions that they're looking
for, but also the diagnosis of the problem.

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:30.799
And if if in a funny way, if I could say what
I think might be the case, even maybe for

00:03:30.799 --> 00:03:32.390
the people that you're talking to.

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:33.390
Yes.

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:37.909
Is that the solutions that they're grasping
for, no matter what part of the spectrum they

00:03:37.909 --> 00:03:42.290
come from, ends up being solutions that would
be associated with the left.

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:47.250
But the diagnosis of the problem sounds a
lot like people from the right.

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:48.530
Mm hmm.

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:51.670
And that's also interesting, right?

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:57.200
Because then we can have a conversation that
can be generative where we can say, Oh, okay,

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.269
so maybe I come from the left, but I can talk
to somebody from the right because actually

00:04:01.269 --> 00:04:06.140
what we could agree on is what's causing some
of these problems.

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:10.950
Even if what we do, even if we disagree on
how we would, what we would do to replace

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:11.950
them.

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:16.070
And that to me, part of the reason why I.
That out.

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:20.060
This different way of seeing things is because
it also I was trying to get people out of

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:27.100
their entrenched teams and get them talking
to each other and finding the the areas of

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:29.030
the landscape on which they might agree.

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:34.660
Speaker 1: One of the things that I've experienced
at the anecdotal personal level when interviewing

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:41.340
former extremists like KKK, neo-Nazi types,
etc., is that they all tell a very similar

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:45.870
story of the circumstances that made them
vulnerable to getting pulled into these movements

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:47.410
in the first place.

00:04:47.410 --> 00:04:49.360
And it's a lot of the things that weren't
working.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:52.770
It was a lot of broken things that that were
going on.

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:58.789
Is this a I mean, not everybody becomes a
KKK member, obviously, or a neo-Nazi, but

00:04:58.789 --> 00:05:04.010
there seems to be a radicalizing effect of
a lot of these things that just aren't working

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:05.010
for people.

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:06.010
Is that accurate?

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:08.820
Speaker 2: I think it is.

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:19.340
And I think the people who study extremism
might find overlapping themes or events in

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:21.680
in some of what I write about.

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:27.340
And, you know, it's part of what's a challenge
here is a friend of mine actually wrote and

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:29.410
said, you know, Elena, I sort of agree with
you.

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:30.690
I see what you're saying.

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:34.250
But I mean, come on, is it really mono causal?

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:38.870
What how did all of these institutions get
broken at the same time?

00:05:38.870 --> 00:05:40.020
How is that even possible?

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:44.360
So many of them were started at different
times.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:47.490
And so, of course, the answer is quite clear,
which is technology.

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:50.600
Like, we did have one thing happen.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:57.550
And if you actually look back to the Industrial
revolution, this exact same thing happened

00:05:57.550 --> 00:05:59.930
in society.

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:05.770
When you have an economic revolution that
changes the means of how we live.

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:11.400
It then comprehensively, almost in a cascade,
changes all parts of life.

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:18.069
It changes religion, communication, the postal
Service, it changes everything.

00:06:18.069 --> 00:06:21.030
And as a result, we have to adjust.

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:23.650
I think the same thing is happening now.

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:25.380
These institute all of our institutions.

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:34.010
We're faced with the challenge of needing
to embrace and adjust themselves to new technologies.

00:06:34.010 --> 00:06:40.460
Some of them adjusted better than others,
and some of them are breaking under the pressure.

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:41.460
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:46.020
Neil Postman wrote quite a bit about this
in his books Technopolis and then also Amusing

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:47.560
Ourselves to Death.

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:51.400
And there's a sort of structural argument
there about, you know, in linguistics there's

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:55.770
this idea it's not I don't know that it's
completely universally accepted, but it's

00:06:55.770 --> 00:07:01.930
much explored that the language you speak
actually impacts your worldview to some degree

00:07:01.930 --> 00:07:07.350
based both on a combination of syntax and
the culture surrounding it in the vocabulary.

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:12.629
The technologies that we have, including social
media or television or whatever, actually

00:07:12.629 --> 00:07:17.539
are deterministic to some degree about what
issues make it into the news media or how

00:07:17.539 --> 00:07:20.410
do we approach problems or whatever the case
may be.

00:07:20.410 --> 00:07:24.690
It's that sort of argument that would explain
the timing, I think, of what you're talking

00:07:24.690 --> 00:07:25.690
about.

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:26.870
Speaker 2: Yes.

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:27.870
Absolutely.

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:33.050
Speaker 1: So Neil Postman would say there's
really no going back.

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:38.961
And it's about how do you try to regulate
in order to harness the parts we want, but

00:07:38.961 --> 00:07:41.879
to limit the parts that don't work so well.

00:07:41.879 --> 00:07:44.000
When we think about solutions, do you agree
with that?

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:48.930
Speaker 2: Yes, I think that part of that,
you know, in this interview, I've talked a

00:07:48.930 --> 00:07:52.830
lot about the brokenness perspective, but
part of what I tried to do with the essay

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:58.470
was also articulate the other perspective,
the sort of Warren perspective, which I also

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:05.740
find incredibly compelling, which is the belief
in what's what exists and the belief that

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:11.490
what exists may or may not need some reforming
or need some tweaking, but that you simply

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:16.770
don't throw whole institutions or systems
out the window, especially when those systems

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:18.270
are.

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:27.270
Ah, the the basket for people's needs sometimes
and their safety and their livelihoods.

00:08:27.270 --> 00:08:35.590
The status quo is believe that we need to
work very, very hard at fixing our current

00:08:35.590 --> 00:08:37.769
institutions.

00:08:37.769 --> 00:08:38.870
In a way.

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:44.540
There's a I think, an incredibly inspiring
and exciting conversation to be had if you

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:45.899
put those two groups together.

00:08:45.899 --> 00:08:51.710
Because what you get is you say, Oh, how about
instead of fighting?

00:08:51.710 --> 00:08:53.930
And trying to convince each other to join
sides.

00:08:53.930 --> 00:08:55.240
We're not going to join.

00:08:55.240 --> 00:09:01.630
What if together we look at the landscape
and we almost assess like, do it, do an assessment

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:04.520
and say, what are the institutions that need
reforming?

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:08.899
What are the institutions that need radical
reforming and what are the institutions that

00:09:08.899 --> 00:09:11.370
need complete replacement?

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:14.070
And let everyone do their part.

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:17.079
Let the brokenness look at something and say,
This is broken.

00:09:17.079 --> 00:09:23.381
I'm burning it to the grounds and I'm starting
again and let the status quo ists reform what

00:09:23.381 --> 00:09:24.810
might actually be salvageable.

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:30.350
Speaker 1: The difficulty in that, as great
as it sounds, is that there is a big divide

00:09:30.350 --> 00:09:35.200
about what's worthy of totally being destroyed
and starting from scratch and what needs to

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:36.200
be reformed.

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:37.770
You mentioned education.

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:43.680
Last week in a 48 hour period, you had Candace
Owens saying the solution to the U.S. being

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:49.030
poor at math is breeding for math skills like
the South Koreans do.

00:09:49.030 --> 00:09:52.300
And then two days later at CPAC, she says
we should get rid of the Department of Education.

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:55.170
It's like you don't need to breed, you need
to teach.

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:56.170
We.

00:09:56.170 --> 00:09:57.170
But they want to.

00:09:57.170 --> 00:10:00.790
But on the left, I think we would not say
we need to get rid of the Department of Education.

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:05.649
So it seems that the disagreement would come
in, which of which institutions fall under

00:10:05.649 --> 00:10:07.370
which of the two categories.

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:09.660
Speaker 2: So you're you're right.

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:15.140
And look, the if the people that you're trying
to create conversation with are people who

00:10:15.140 --> 00:10:18.010
are political rhetoric, missions, that's a
challenge.

00:10:18.010 --> 00:10:19.950
It's very different from my own right.

00:10:19.950 --> 00:10:28.220
I'm actually looking to create to generate
a conversation that feels fruitful among.

00:10:28.220 --> 00:10:29.600
Regular people, okay?

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:34.649
People who actually are maybe people who belong
to the same town.

00:10:34.649 --> 00:10:35.649
Right.

00:10:35.649 --> 00:10:40.410
But who feel differently about the solutions
or differently about the institutions of their

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:42.300
world.

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:49.180
And they're invested at a personal and granular
level in those institutions.

00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:52.730
And they are not grand standers who are just
looking to make broad points.

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:55.520
These are people who actually want to get
their hands dirty.

00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:57.190
They just don't know in what way.

00:10:57.190 --> 00:11:02.820
They don't know if they're if they want to
get their hands dirty by.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:06.399
Completely abandoning their local school district.

00:11:06.399 --> 00:11:08.540
Because it's become too corrupt.

00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:09.540
Right.

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:12.750
Or by reforming it, getting different people
on the board.

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:17.529
And those are to me, those are decisions that
actually do have to get made at the local

00:11:17.529 --> 00:11:24.970
level and do have to get made person by person,
town by town, community by community.

00:11:24.970 --> 00:11:30.029
And then I think it generates friends nationally
and maybe even internationally.

00:11:30.029 --> 00:11:32.589
Speaker 1: Super, super interesting.

00:11:32.589 --> 00:11:37.120
We've been speaking with Alana Newhouse, who's
the founder and editor in chief of Tablet

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:38.120
magazine.

00:11:38.120 --> 00:11:42.139
And we'll link to the articles that we've
been sort of talking about in this conversation.

00:11:42.139 --> 00:11:44.540
Alana, really appreciate your time and insights.

00:11:44.540 --> 00:11:46.619
Speaker 2: Thanks so much for having me.

